    description: "An 80-question, 80-mark original Verbal Reasoning paper built to the FULL 15-block EBMC-paper structure (the same catalogue used by vr-intensive-diagnostic/mock-2/mock-3 — Verbal Analogies, Number Sequences, Logic Puzzles, Letter Values, Opposites, Letter Sequences, Word Bridges, Hidden Words, Odd One Out, Move a Letter, Word Codes, Number Equations, Insert Letter, Compound Words, Number Codes — see research/ebmc-vr-paper-format-breakdown.md), not the narrower 6-block Verbal-Analogies/Opposites/Synonyms/Odd-One-Out/Logic-Puzzles/Number-Analogies structure used by mock-4/-4-easier/-5/-6. Block order (with each block's question count, a fresh reshuffle distinct from the diagnostic/mock-2/mock-3 orders): Letter Values (5), Word Bridges (5), Verbal Analogies (7, two-group pick), Number Codes (4), Odd One Out where exactly two of five must be marked (5), Insert Letter (6), Opposites (6, two-group pick), Number Sequences (6), Hidden Words spanning a word boundary (6), a Logic/Statement-Reasoning puzzle (2), Word Codes (5), Compound Words (6, two-group pick), Move a Letter (6), Letter Sequences (6), Number Equations (5). Difficulty matches vr-intensive-mock-6's band: advanced 11+ Elite vocabulary in the word-based blocks (mendacious, magnanimous, phlegmatic, vindictive, querulous, torpid, parsimonious, nefarious, erudite, ebullient, and their antonym/synonym partners), entirely fresh from every earlier vrb/vrc/vrd/vre/vrn/vro/vrp/vrs-prefixed question in the series (no shared words, sentences, sequences, or ciphers). Every two-group-pick Analogies/Opposites/Compound-Words question (19 total) uses deliberately unrelated filler words (materials, colours, numbers, shapes, drawn from mismatched domains between the two groups) as decoys rather than same-domain real-world facts, specifically to avoid the two documented two-group ambiguity failure patterns (an intra-group synonym cluster, or a decoy whose real relationship partner sits in the other group) — early drafts of several Analogies questions using real-world profession/animal-feature decoys (e.g. sculptor/painter tool-vs-material-vs-product, baby-animal habitat/sound/colour decoys) were caught and rejected during authoring specifically because animals and professions have multiple simultaneously-valid analogy dimensions, making mismatched-domain filler words the only reliably safe decoy choice for this format. Every Odd One Out (mark-two-format) question's correctAnswer was written pre-sorted in alphabetical order, and every Logic Puzzle was traced by hand to confirm exactly one statement is forced true across all valid arrangements (vrf85's puzzle has two valid full arrangements, both checked). Every Number Codes/Word Codes/Number Equations/Letter Values/Letter Sequences answer was independently recomputed letter-by-letter or digit-by-digit before being finalised. **This mock also newly documents a real, previously-unrecorded systemic bug found live in this same session**: `MarkTwoAnswer` (the Odd One Out UI component) always alphabetically-sorts the student's two picks before saving, but `isCorrect()` does a plain string compare with no re-sorting — so any mark-two-format question whose authored `correctAnswer` isn't itself alphabetically sorted silently marks a fully correct student answer wrong. This was found affecting 23 questions across vr-intensive-mock-4/-4-easier/-5/-6 (Lupin lost marks to it on a live mock-6 attempt), is now written up as Bug 7 in research/mock-authoring-lessons.md, and this mock's own Odd One Out answers were built sort-order-safe from the start. Original Summit Tuition content inspired by the real paper's question-format structure, not a reproduction of its actual questions. Seeded live and unlocked directly for Lupin per explicit founder instruction this session (a departure from the usual 'not yet seeded, founder reviews first' convention).",
