    description: "The fourth full-length, Elite-difficulty English paper matching the real GL Assessment 11+ structure exactly: 28 reading comprehension questions on the original passage 'The Lighthouse Keeper's Ledger', 9 spelling and 9 grammar-mistake questions in the authentic segment 'find the mistake, or choose N' format, and 8 cloze questions. Fully re-authored on 2026-08-06 (every one of the 54 questions rewritten in place; question ids, section split and total marks unchanged) after a real marked student report showed a strong candidate scoring 83-85% on the harder papers in this series while sitting at or near 100% on comprehension — the previous version of this paper, written at the same level as the original four Elite English papers, had become far too easy for that student. Section A now follows the platform's no-easy-elimination rule strictly: every retrieval question draws at least two of its wrong options from other genuinely true details elsewhere in the same passage (a fact from a different paragraph, an action performed by the other character, a qualified statement with its qualifier removed), so no question can be answered by ruling out invented-sounding options; several retrieval items now turn on a single qualifying phrase ('alone after dark', 'without gripping the rail with both hands') rather than on locating a sentence. The eight inference questions lean on real-world social reasoning and human motivation rather than lookup: why a man begins an explanation twice and abandons it twice, what a hand that tightens and then goes completely still is actually doing, what 'he told himself this most nights without quite believing it' concedes about a character's self-deception, why a name is written 'without quite deciding to', and why the ledger finally handed over is a blank new one rather than the old one — each with one or two wrong options that are plausible over-reads of the same evidence, and each still resolving to a single textually anchored answer. Vocabulary-in-context moved from plain synonym checks to six words whose contextual sense differs from a strong everyday-meaning distractor ('restlessness' of the sea, 'rhythm' of a lamp, 'still' as a verb, 'closed' of an entry, 'shape' of trust, 'generously' as quantity rather than warmth). Sections B-D were rebuilt to abandon the five generic templated subtopics this project's notes warn against. Grammar-mistake now uses nine specifically named niche points not used by any other mock on the platform: 'comprise' wrongly followed by 'of', 'between... and' misused for a continuous range, 'could of' for 'could have', existential 'there is' with a plural complement, 'neither' wrongly paired with 'or', 'like' as a conjunction in place of 'as', 'hardly... than' for 'hardly... when', 'prefer... than' for 'prefer... to', and a genuine no-mistake item on 'none' taking a singular verb. Cloze — the section the student's real report showed as weakest, at 2/8 — now tests fronted 'scarcely' with inversion and the past perfect, 'but for' as an unreal past condition, the inverted 'had it not been for', inversion plus agreement after a fronted 'so' plus adjective, the meaning shift between 'stop doing' and 'stop to do', 'few' versus 'a few', 'be used to' plus a gerund, and 'it is high time' plus the past subjunctive. Spelling moved off the standard trap-word list to liaison, embarrassed, rhythm, questionnaire, acquaintance, perseverance and manoeuvre, with two no-mistake items on the homophone sets principal/principle, stationery/stationary and complement/compliment. Calibrated so a student scoring 83-85% on english-gl-16-elite-plus and english-gl-19-ultra lands around 70% here: roughly 5-7 marks expected to be lost across the hardened comprehension section, 1-2 on spelling, 2-3 on grammar-mistake and 4-5 on cloze. Note for future sessions: questions eh300-eh314 are also reused by the pates-grammar-style diagnostic mock, so that paper's English section became harder as a side effect of this rewrite; ids were kept in place deliberately, per the founder's instruction not to renumber. Not copied from any third-party paper — 100% original Summit Tuition content.",
